Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Turntable Swallows the Blizzard
- Story & Context: When the supply locomotive derailed outside the Narvik coastal railhead during the brutal blizzard of December '97, stationmaster Dagny Lind refused to freeze in the dark, dragging an emergency generator and a pair of 909 samplers onto the stalled railyard turntable. As the iron turntable began spinning in the howling wind, she miked the shrieking steel bearings and blended them with hand-held salt shakers over the station’s shortwave public address horn to rally the stranded rail hands into a wild stomp. The crew spent six hours dancing in circles on the frozen rail tracks until the engine thawed, shouting the chorus into the gale: "If the iron engine freezes then our blood will turn to fire!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An expansive cinematic painting of an old iron rail turntable spinning in an arctic blizzard, throwing sparks and golden incandescent stage-light into falling snow, with dozens of rail workers in thick parkas dancing around high-voltage equipment while a massive locomotive rests frosted on the tracks.

Track 2: Who Stole the Timetable Off Track Zero?
- Story & Context: Recorded in the cramped radio shack of the Hammerfest outpost, this manic confessional was Torstein’s direct response to losing his official train departure charts in an engine fire, leaving him utterly oblivious to when the last ferry-car would ever arrive from the south. Rather than panic, Torstein spent the polar night chugging black coffee and tracking 210-BPM syncopated shaker-fills using a rusted flour sieve filled with dried sea-beans, screaming his relief over blown-out radio fuzz. The resulting vocal cut became the unofficial anthem for northern rail jumpers who deliberately chose exile over returning home: "Forget the printed hours because we’re never punching out!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A gritty, surreal close-up shot inside a steam-filled train depot office at the edge of the world; scattered paper timetables burn inside a cast-iron stove, while illuminated vacuum-tube radio rigs glow beside a frosted window looking out onto empty snowbound railroad tracks.

Track 3: Fyrvakt-Jubileum
- Story & Context: The final track commemorates the legendary 1999 winter equinox broadcast, when seven disconnected arctic lighthouses simultaneously locked their radio frequencies to transmit one continuous, sun-summoning shaker cascade across five hundred miles of frozen ocean. Fishermen off Bear Island reported seeing the lighthouse beams converge into a golden web in the clouds while the ship radio sang with deafening, joyful synth leads that drove entire crews to bang wrenches against their hulls in ecstatic accompaniment. By the time the track climaxed with a wall of overdriven foghorns, dawn finally cracked the horizon for the first time in ninety days, immortalizing the hook: "Turn the glass, strike the brass, daylight found the coast at last!"
